ndpoint  every project becomes back-ended just  how back-ended a project becomes as  influenced by a number of variables  including script rewrites character  environment changes in decision you get  the point almost always though the  delivery date never moves so any of  these changes result in more work to  complete in a shorter amount of time and  something has to give in a perfect world  you work on multiple projects and they  time out nicely so you get good  utilization of your render farm and  maintain a steady artist base here we  look at three features running in the  same year each spread out in a  manageable way in a world like this you  can buy the rendering cores you need and  it's a good investment and this is kind  of what Todd was referring to earlier  lately though things have not been so  ideal and projects are piling up on one  another creating hiring challenges and  big resource shortfalls and this is  before anything unexpected happens on  one or more of the projects which is  something you can certainly count on  happening this has become the new normal  within entertainment so where does the  cloud come in well there's really two  parts to this one's defensive and the  other offensive on the defensive side  our resource deficits have become so  large that traditional procurement is  simply too expensive these shows require  upwards of 10 million dollars or more in  additional resources for periods as  short as four to six weeks in order to  finish the project at this scale few  companies are willing and/or able to  rent bare metal servers for such short  periods of time again we're talking a  few weeks here anyone who's willing to  rent wants to rent for multiple years  not weeks and the price is generally  very expensive the on-demand burst model  of cloud fits with our sporadic capacity  and flexibility requirements without us  having to make any commitment our  projects change literally from day to  day so that flexibility is really  important to us lastly with cloud  there's no physical infrastructure to  manage  which cuts down on the ancillary costs  associated with logistics installation  in ongoing operation on the offensive  end is speed looking back at the artists  cycle the quicker recycle the fewer  artists we need or the more work we can  take in or the more iterations we get  and we might even get a better picture  we also feel strongly that our  infrastructure should never be the  slowest link it's actually the one area  we have complete control and if we can  scale up resources to see if artists  time or give our clients what they want  when they want it we'll gladly do so so  why use the Google cloud platform well  we tried other platforms at scale and we  found success using them but for us  Google sticks out in a few ways a key  differentiator for us is Google's highly  performant network and infrastructure  Google really likes to point this out to  customers as Todd did earlier I always  thought of course they're gonna say this  why wouldn't they say this but it really  wasn't until we ran its scale and had  experience using other platforms that we  truly understood what they meant and it  turns out that they were right Google's  P VMs fit well into our rendering  methodology we launched an n1 standard  16 or an n1 standard 30 to PVM per frame  and all machines are the same meaning we  don't have to take what's available or  manage complicated bidding strategies we  choose what we want when we want and if  we ever need something custom we request  it and we pay a little bit more the  Google Cloud is simple straightforward  and for us has proved to cost as much as  50% less than other alternatives in the  market lastly the Google team  understands our business because the  network of support they've built is made  up of people who come from our business  and that's really important to us so  let's take a look closer look at our GCP  pipeline today our GCP pipeline is  modeled to support